1. Independent Gaussian weights, relative squared error at R bits per weight
   levels | bits | floor for any code, 2^(-2R) | best scalar quantiser (Lloyd-Max) | best evenly spaced levels | its step in standard deviations
        3 | 1.585 | 0.11111 = -9.54 dB | 0.19017 = -7.21 dB | 0.19017 = -7.21 dB | 1.2240
        4 | 2.000 | 0.06250 = -12.04 dB | 0.11748 = -9.30 dB | 0.11885 = -9.25 dB | 0.9957
        8 | 3.000 | 0.01562 = -18.06 dB | 0.03455 = -14.62 dB | 0.03744 = -14.27 dB | 0.5860
       16 | 4.000 | 0.00391 = -24.08 dB | 0.00950 = -20.22 dB | 0.01154 = -19.38 dB | 0.3352
   The first column of numbers is Shannon's rate-distortion function: a proved floor for block codes of any length.
   The second is the floor for any code that rounds one weight at a time to a fixed number of levels.
   Both are floors on the expected error. One finite matrix can fall a little under them.

2. What the shape of the cells is worth when the cells are fine and nothing is clipped
   Error is G times the cell volume to the power 2/n. Gain is against the cube, G = 1/12. One bit is 6.02 dB.
   cells | G | gain in dB | gain in bits per weight
   cube, any dimension                          | 0.083333 | 0.000 | 0.0000
   A_2* (hexagons)                              | 0.080188 | 0.167 | 0.0278
   A_3*                                         | 0.078543 | 0.257 | 0.0427
   A_4*                                         | 0.077559 | 0.312 | 0.0518
   D_4                                          | 0.076603 | 0.366 | 0.0607
   A_8*                                         | 0.075972 | 0.402 | 0.0667
   E_8                                          | 0.071682 | 0.654 | 0.1086
   ball in 8 dimensions (no tiling reaches it)  | 0.070454 | 0.729 | 0.1211
   ball in 24 dimensions                        | 0.064750 | 1.096 | 0.1820
   limit of the ball, 1 / (2 pi e)              | 0.058550 | 1.533 | 0.2546
   The ball rows are ceilings: no partition of space into cells of equal volume has a smaller G in that dimension.

3. The closed form used for A_n*, checked
   G(A_n*) = (n+1)^(1/n) / 12 * (1 - (Q(n+1) - 1) / n), with Q(m) = sum over k = 1..m of m! / ((m-k)! m^k).
   n = 1: formula 0.083333333, known exact value 0.083333333, difference 0.0e+00
   n = 2: formula 0.080187537, known exact value 0.080187537, difference 1.4e-17
   n = 3: formula 0.078543281, known exact value 0.078543281, difference 1.4e-17
   n = 4: formula 0.077559, measured with the nearest-point decoder 0.077558 +- 0.000015 (-0.1 standard errors)
   n = 8: formula 0.075972, measured with the nearest-point decoder 0.075972 +- 0.000010 (+0.1 standard errors)
   E_8: exact 929/12960 = 0.071682, measured 0.071685 +- 0.000008 (+0.4 standard errors)
   The formula was fitted here to the exact values for n = 1, 2, 3 and then tested at n = 4 and 8. Its source has not been opened.

4. Covering thickness: the mean number of balls that cover a point. 1 would be a covering with no overlap
   n | cubic grid | A_n* | note
   2 | 1.5708 | 1.2092 | A_2* is the thinnest covering of the plane
   3 | 2.7207 | 1.4635 | A_3* is the thinnest lattice covering
   4 | 4.9348 | 1.7655 | thinnest lattice covering
   5 | 9.1955 | 2.1243 | thinnest lattice covering
   8 | 64.9394 | 3.6658 | E_8 has 4.0587; the best lattice known has 3.1422, a record and not a bound (Schurmann and Vallentin 2006)
   E_8 computed here: ball volume in 8 dimensions = 4.0587

5. Exact size of a codebook cut from a lattice by a box, by inclusion-exclusion
   Vectors of 9 integers with sum zero (the lattice A_8) and every entry between -k and k.
   k | codewords | bits per weight for 8 weights
   1 | 3139 | 1.4520
   2 | 180325 | 2.1825
   3 | 2636263 | 2.6663
   4 | 19610233 | 3.0281
   A Voronoi code needs none of this: it has exactly q^n codewords, log2(q) bits per weight.

6. Packing ternary weights
   5 ternary weights fit in one byte: 243 of 256 states, 99.06 percent of the byte's 8 bits, 1.60 bits stored per weight against log2(3) = 1.5850

7. The point set F_n of Foundation-0/incentre-lattices as a codebook (proposed in PR #6): what its cells are worth
   The cube [-1, 1]^n is cut into the 2n pyramids over its facets, with one point on the axis of each at distance t from the centre.
   The cell of each point is its pyramid for any 0 < t < 1 (proof in experiments/incentre-vortex-codebooks-2026-11-17/README.md).
   G = [n/(n+2) - 2t n/(n+1) + t^2 + n(n-1)/(3(n+2))] / (n (2^n / 2n)^(2/n)). A negative gain means worse than the cube.
   n | G at t = 2 - sqrt 2 (the incentre) | gain in dB | G at t = n/(n+1) (the centroid, the best t) | gain in dB | measured at the centroid
    2 | 0.114382 | -1.375 | 0.111111 | -1.249 | 0.111128 +- 0.000070 (+0.2 standard errors)
    3 | 0.127803 | -1.857 | 0.120383 | -1.597 | 0.120376 +- 0.000062 (-0.1 standard errors)
    4 | 0.130677 | -1.954 | 0.122565 | -1.675 | 0.122494 +- 0.000053 (-1.3 standard errors)
    8 | 0.123026 | -1.692 | 0.117284 | -1.484 | 0.117232 +- 0.000033 (-1.6 standard errors)
   16 | 0.110216 | -1.214 | 0.107172 | -1.093 | 0.107153 +- 0.000019 (-1.0 standard errors)
   24 | 0.103819 | -0.955 | 0.101805 | -0.869 | 0.101793 +- 0.000015 (-0.8 standard errors)
   F_n is behind the cube in every dimension. Its G tends to 1/12 from above as n grows.

8. Lattices in more than 8 dimensions: the best known, from a source and not recomputed here
   lattice | dimension | G | gain in dB against the cube | gain in dB against E_8
   K_12 (Coxeter-Todd)      | 12 | 0.070095600 | 0.75 | 0.10
   Lambda_16 (Barnes-Wall)  | 16 | 0.06830 | 0.86 | 0.21
   Lambda_24 (Leech)        | 24 | 0.06577 | 1.03 | 0.37
   Source: Agrell and Allen, arXiv 2202.09605, table I, with the digits it prints. K_12 is exact there and the other two are numerical.
   These are records and not ceilings. The ceilings are the ball rows of section 2.

9. Three dimensions: the end point of the fold that PR #6 proposes as a training schedule is the face-centred cubic lattice D_3
   D_3, measured with the nearest-point decoder: G = 0.078724 +- 0.000018, 0.247 dB against the cube
   ball in 3 dimensions (no tiling reaches it): G = 0.076967, 0.345 dB against the cube
   E_8 is worth 0.654 dB. So no cells in 3 dimensions are worth what the E_8 cell is worth.

10. The region of a code when one scale serves a group and nothing is clipped (issue #10): Gaussian weights, from integrals
   With fine cells: error / variance = G * Phi * codewords^(-2/n). G is the cell (section 2). Phi is the region:
   Phi = volume(K)^(2/n) * E[square of the largest K-norm of a block in a group of g weights] / variance, for a region of shape K.
   Region gain = 10 log10 of the cube's Phi over the region's: what the region is worth against the scalar largest-entry rule.
   g | Phi of the cube | ball in 8 dimensions: Phi | its gain in dB | cross-polytope in 8 dimensions: Phi | its gain in dB | ceiling in 8 dimensions: ball region and ball cell, in dB
      8 | 13.830 | 11.355 | +0.856 | 12.311 | +0.505 | +1.585
     32 | 22.838 | 17.537 | +1.147 | 19.431 | +0.702 | +1.876
    128 | 32.593 | 23.421 | +1.435 | 26.465 | +0.905 | +2.164
    512 | 42.700 | 28.931 | +1.691 | 33.246 | +1.087 | +2.420
   2048 | 53.005 | 34.155 | +1.909 | 39.822 | +1.242 | +2.638
   Measured on three Gaussian matrices in the record e8-gain-largest-entry-rule-2026-11-17: ball +0.85, +1.15, +1.44 and +1.90 dB
   at g = 8, 32, 128 and 2048, and cross-polytope +0.70 and +1.24 dB at g = 32 and 2048.
   The ball column is a ceiling for round clouds. If the blocks are spread evenly over all directions, no region has a smaller Phi
   than the ball (proof in experiments/shape-formula-held-out-2026-11-17/PREREG.md). So by this formula no code in 8 dimensions with one scale per group gains
   more over the scalar rule on Gaussian weights than the last column.

   For any weights whatever, not only Gaussian ones. For a block of 8 weights,
   (largest entry)^2 <= squared length <= (sum of sizes)^2 <= 8 * squared length, and squared length <= 8 * (largest entry)^2.
   The largest over a group and the mean over groups keep these. So the gain of one region over another lies between two fixed numbers:
   ball over cube             | -4.531 dB to +4.500 dB
   cross-polytope over cube   | -6.548 dB to +11.514 dB
   cross-polytope over ball   | -2.017 dB to +7.014 dB
   The upper end is 2.5 log10 of the ratio of the two volumes at equal reach along a weight axis. It is reached when every block
   that sets a scale holds one weight and seven zeros. The lower end is reached when such a block has eight equal weights.
   shape/check_certificate.py checks the identities behind the three inequalities.

   The same against Shannon's floor 2^(-2B) at B bits per weight in total, with 16 bits per scale: 10 log10(G Phi) + 6.02 * 16 / g, in dB above the floor
   g | scalar rule | E_8 in a ball | ball cell in a ball (the ceiling in 8 dimensions)
      8 | 12.66 | 11.15 | 11.07
     32 | 5.81 | 4.00 | 3.93
    128 | 5.09 | 3.00 | 2.93
    512 | 5.70 | 3.36 | 3.28
   2048 | 6.50 | 3.94 | 3.86
   A small group pays for its scale and a large one for its largest block. Of these five, groups of 128 give the smallest error at equal bits.

   More dimensions, one group per row of 2016 weights: the gain of a ball region over the scalar rule, in dB
   n = 8: +1.906, n = 16: +2.637, n = 24: +3.028. Measured in that record on Gaussian matrices: +1.90, +2.62 and +3.02.
